data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- Cas9, a protein, cuts DNA at the location identified by a guide RNA, which binds the target DNA [1].\n- A synthetic guide RNA delivered with Cas9 into a cell lets the genome be cut at a desired location, so existing genes can be removed or new ones added [7].\n- Scientists commonly use CRISPR to \"knock out\" a gene by breaking it, so the cell stops making the protein it codes for [3].\n- Adding a DNA fragment as a repair template can replace a disease-causing mutation with a healthy sequence [3].\n- A reported study claims HIV in cell culture can be completely eliminated using CRISPR-Cas gene editing [6]."}

