data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"analyst","content":"- **Programmable cutting:** Cas9 is a protein that cuts DNA wherever a guide RNA directs it to bind [1]. A synthetic guide RNA delivered with Cas9 lets researchers cut the genome at a chosen spot [7].

- **Gene knockout and repair:** Breaking a gene stops the cell from making its protein, which is how scientists commonly knock out genes [3]. A DNA repair template can also replace a disease-causing mutation with a healthy sequence [3].

- **Unverified claim:** A reported study says CRISPR-Cas editing can completely eliminate HIV in cell culture [6]. This is a single reported claim, so it needs independent confirmation."}

data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"critic","content":"- **Off-target risk is missing:** Nothing covers unintended cuts at similar DNA sequences, a central limitation of the programmable cutting claims.\n- **Single-source support:** The guide RNA claim rests on one source, and the knockout and repair claims both rely on the same single source.\n- **Overstated HIV claim:** \"Completely eliminate\" needs qualifying. The review should note that cell-culture results don't establish clinical effectiveness."}

